WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.140
Cortisol tells the liver to make more glucose.

2
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:12.080
The adrenal releases it in pulses, roughly every sixty to ninety minutes.

3
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.040
Does its level in the blood pulse too?

4
00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:21.090
The first film built a tank, with a gland's tap pouring in and a drain clearing out.

5
00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:23.970
The level settles where the two match.

6
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:26.270
Now we time the drain.

7
00:00:28.083 --> 00:00:32.583
You Shut the tap. The level falls, but not in a straight line.

8
00:00:33.883 --> 00:00:36.383
The drain removes a constant fraction each minute.

9
00:00:37.583 --> 00:00:41.083
So the level halves, then halves again, in equal steps of time.

10
00:00:42.423 --> 00:00:45.183
Wherever it starts, each halving takes the same time.

11
00:00:46.217 --> 00:00:49.137
The time for one halving is the hormone's half-life.

12
00:00:50.377 --> 00:00:54.717
In this example, a small peptide hormone has a half-life of five minutes.

13
00:00:55.957 --> 00:00:59.477
For cortisol, textbooks give about sixty to ninety minutes.

14
00:01:00.483 --> 00:01:03.063
Now open the tap again and leave it open.

15
00:01:04.263 --> 00:01:08.343
The level climbs toward a new steady value, where the drain matches the tap.

16
00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:11.443
It gets halfway there in one half-life.

17
00:01:12.743 --> 00:01:14.683
It is nearly there after about four.

18
00:01:15.733 --> 00:01:18.553
So the half-life sets the speed in both directions.

19
00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:23.213
A slow drain means a slow fall, and also a slow rise.

20
00:01:24.493 --> 00:01:26.593
The level always lags behind the tap.

21
00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:31.553
A short half-life lets a level answer its gland within minutes.

22
00:01:32.753 --> 00:01:33.973
A long one takes hours.

23
00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:37.187
Now give two hormones the same tap.

24
00:01:38.427 --> 00:01:41.807
It opens in equal short bursts, like cortisol's pulses.

25
00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:44.887
One hormone has a fast drain.

26
00:01:46.227 --> 00:01:48.167
The other has cortisol's slow drain.

27
00:01:49.217 --> 00:01:50.857
Same bursts, two drains.

28
00:01:50.857 --> 00:01:54.057
Before they run, make your call.

29
00:01:54.837 --> 00:01:58.817
Compared with its own average, which level will swing more with each burst?

30
00:02:05.233 --> 00:02:07.913
Each burst adds the same amount to both tanks.

31
00:02:09.133 --> 00:02:12.493
The fast-draining level then empties before the next burst.

32
00:02:13.813 --> 00:02:16.533
So each burst lifts it far above its tiny average.

33
00:02:17.833 --> 00:02:20.013
The slow-draining level never empties.

34
00:02:21.173 --> 00:02:23.573
It ripples gently around a much higher middle.

35
00:02:24.633 --> 00:02:29.433
So with brief bursts, what matters is the half-life compared with the gap between

36
00:02:29.433 --> 00:02:33.253
them. Much shorter, and the level tracks each pulse.

37
00:02:34.593 --> 00:02:37.033
As long as the gap, and the level smooths them.

38
00:02:38.233 --> 00:02:43.193
In this model, cortisol's level still ripples with each pulse, but it never empties.

39
00:02:44.267 --> 00:02:46.527
One rule still holds over many bursts.

40
00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:50.587
On average, the drain removes what the tap adds.

41
00:02:51.887 --> 00:02:54.887
A slow drain removes a smaller fraction each minute.

42
00:02:55.987 --> 00:02:57.967
So it needs a fuller tank to keep up.

43
00:02:59.087 --> 00:03:01.527
That is why the slow-draining level sits higher.

44
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.400
These curves come from a simple model with invented numbers.

45
00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.640
Real secretion varies over time.

46
00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.800
It is rarely one steady rate, or switched off outright.
